{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with the comparison a reader must make. A chart type is the result of\nthat decision, not the starting point."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts does not inspect a dataset and choose a chart automatically.\nData exploration, cleaning, and recommendation belong in application tooling\nor an authoring skill. The runtime receives data that is ready to encode."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"task-first-choices","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Task-first choices"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reader task"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First choice"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common alternatives"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Follow change over ordered time"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Line"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Area for accumulated magnitude; bars for discrete periods"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare named categories"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Horizontal bars"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dots or lollipops when a zero baseline is not the message"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare two values per category"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dumbbell or slopegraph"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouped bars when absolute magnitude matters"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inspect a relationship"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scatterplot"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bubble scatterplot for a third quantitative value"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inspect one distribution"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Histogram"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ECDF when cumulative probability matters"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare distributions"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boxplot or violin"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faceted histograms when shape details matter"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Show an interval"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Range area, interval bar, or error bar"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Candlestick for financial open-high-low-close data"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Show composition"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stacked bars or areas"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Normalized stack for proportions; mosaic for two categorical dimensions"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Show a matrix"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Heatmap"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Labeled cells for small matrices"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Repeat the same view by group"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Facets"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Linked views when each panel needs a different role"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Show topology"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Node-link diagram"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Matrix encoding when the network is dense"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Edit or navigate a time range"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chart plus semantic controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled brush or zoom behavior; application-owned scrubber or editor"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"chart examples"}]},{"type":"text","value":" show these choices as executable\ncompositions."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"check-the-data-shape","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check the data shape"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before choosing marks, identify:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the observation represented by one row;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"quantitative, temporal, ordinal, and nominal fields;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"whether missing values mean unknown, zero, or a deliberate gap;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"whether order is semantic or merely presentational;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"which rows share a series or group;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"whether values are points or intervals;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"whether aggregation would answer the question more clearly than raw marks."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep those rows in their natural application shape. Use\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/data-and-channels.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"channels"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to map fields or accessors into\nvisual properties. Do not convert data into a library-owned series structure."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"prefer-the-smallest-complete-composition","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer the smallest complete composition"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use this escalation order:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One built-in mark or first-party composite mark."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Several built-in marks sharing scales."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Facets or explicitly linked views."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"D3-prepared rows passed to built-in marks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compositeMark"},{"type":"text","value":" for a reusable unit made only from ordinary marks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A custom mark that emits scene nodes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An application-owned overlay or gesture controller."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer a first-party mark when it owns inseparable semantics. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxX"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxY"},{"type":"text","value":"\naccept raw observations and keep quartiles, fences, whiskers, outliers, and\nlineage aligned. A candlestick remains a useful link-plus-ranged-rectangle\ncomposition. A focus-and-context view is two charts sharing semantic state."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/marks-and-layering.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks and Layering"}]},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./faceting-and-composition.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faceting and Composition"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./custom-marks-and-renderers.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Marks and Renderers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the\ncorresponding boundaries."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"avoid-misleading-defaults","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Avoid misleading defaults"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar charts should normally include zero on the quantitative axis."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Line charts imply an ordered continuum. Do not connect unordered categories."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Area encodings compare distance from a baseline. Use explicit interval\nendpoints when the baseline is not zero."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stacks make totals easy to compare but interior layers harder to compare."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bubble radius must use an area-preserving radial scale."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dual quantitative axes often make unrelated movement look related. Prefer\naligned small multiples."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Three-dimensional effects distort position, length, and area. Keep analytical\nmarks planar."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"More raw marks are not automatically more honest. For dense data, choose a\nbounded representation that preserves the question."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"./large-data.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Large Data"}]},{"type":"text","value":" guide covers the raw-versus-encoded decision."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"verify-the-result","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verify the result"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A chart is ready when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"its title or surrounding text states the question;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the axes and legend identify units;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ordering and aggregation are intentional;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"color is not the only carrier of essential state;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"missing and empty states are explicit;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"focus and keyboard behavior match pointer behavior;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"updates preserve keys, selection, and viewport state;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the chart remains readable at its smallest supported container;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a table or textual summary is available when exact values are essential."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the first attempt fails one of these checks, change the representation\nbefore adding decorative complexity."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"task-first-choices","text":"Task-first choices","level":2},{"id":"check-the-data-shape","text":"Check the data shape","level":2},{"id":"prefer-the-smallest-complete-composition","text":"Prefer the smallest complete composition","level":2},{"id":"avoid-misleading-defaults","text":"Avoid misleading defaults","level":2},{"id":"verify-the-result","text":"Verify the result","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nStart with the comparison a reader must make. A chart type is the result of\nthat decision, not the starting point.\n\nTanStack Charts does not inspect a dataset and choose a chart automatically.\nData exploration, cleaning, and recommendation belong in application tooling\nor an authoring skill. The runtime receives data that is ready to encode.\n\n## Task-first choices\n\n| Reader task                     | First choice                           | Common alternatives                                                     |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Follow change over ordered time | Line                                   | Area for accumulated magnitude; bars for discrete periods               |\n| Compare named categories        | Horizontal bars                        | Dots or lollipops when a zero baseline is not the message               |\n| Compare two values per category | Dumbbell or slopegraph                 | Grouped bars when absolute magnitude matters                            |\n| Inspect a relationship          | Scatterplot                            | Bubble scatterplot for a third quantitative value                       |\n| Inspect one distribution        | Histogram                              | ECDF when cumulative probability matters                                |\n| Compare distributions           | Boxplot or violin                      | Faceted histograms when shape details matter                            |\n| Show an interval                | Range area, interval bar, or error bar | Candlestick for financial open-high-low-close data                      |\n| Show composition                | Stacked bars or areas                  | Normalized stack for proportions; mosaic for two categorical dimensions |\n| Show a matrix                   | Heatmap                                | Labeled cells for small matrices                                        |\n| Repeat the same view by group   | Facets                                 | Linked views when each panel needs a different role                     |\n| Show topology                   | Node-link diagram                      | Matrix encoding when the network is dense                               |\n| Edit or navigate a time range   | Chart plus semantic controls           | Controlled brush or zoom behavior; application-owned scrubber or editor |\n\nThe [chart examples](../examples/index.md) show these choices as executable\ncompositions.\n\n## Check the data shape\n\nBefore choosing marks, identify:\n\n- the observation represented by one row;\n- quantitative, temporal, ordinal, and nominal fields;\n- whether missing values mean unknown, zero, or a deliberate gap;\n- whether order is semantic or merely presentational;\n- which rows share a series or group;\n- whether values are points or intervals;\n- whether aggregation would answer the question more clearly than raw marks.\n\nKeep those rows in their natural application shape. Use\n[channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md) to map fields or accessors into\nvisual properties. Do not convert data into a library-owned series structure.\n\n## Prefer the smallest complete composition\n\nUse this escalation order:\n\n1. One built-in mark or first-party composite mark.\n2. Several built-in marks sharing scales.\n3. Facets or explicitly linked views.\n4. D3-prepared rows passed to built-in marks.\n5. `compositeMark` for a reusable unit made only from ordinary marks.\n6. A custom mark that emits scene nodes.\n7. An application-owned overlay or gesture controller.\n\nPrefer a first-party mark when it owns inseparable semantics. `boxX` and `boxY`\naccept raw observations and keep quartiles, fences, whiskers, outliers, and\nlineage aligned. A candlestick remains a useful link-plus-ranged-rectangle\ncomposition. A focus-and-context view is two charts sharing semantic state.\n\nSee [Marks and Layering](../concepts/marks-and-layering.md),\n[Faceting and Composition](./faceting-and-composition.md), and\n[Custom Marks and Renderers](./custom-marks-and-renderers.md) for the\ncorresponding boundaries.\n\n## Avoid misleading defaults\n\n- Bar charts should normally include zero on the quantitative axis.\n- Line charts imply an ordered continuum. Do not connect unordered categories.\n- Area encodings compare distance from a baseline. Use explicit interval\n  endpoints when the baseline is not zero.\n- Stacks make totals easy to compare but interior layers harder to compare.\n- Bubble radius must use an area-preserving radial scale.\n- Dual quantitative axes often make unrelated movement look related. Prefer\n  aligned small multiples.\n- Three-dimensional effects distort position, length, and area. Keep analytical\n  marks planar.\n- More raw marks are not automatically more honest. For dense data, choose a\n  bounded representation that preserves the question.\n\nThe [Large Data](./large-data.md) guide covers the raw-versus-encoded decision.\n\n## Verify the result\n\nA chart is ready when:\n\n- its title or surrounding text states the question;\n- the axes and legend identify units;\n- ordering and aggregation are intentional;\n- color is not the only carrier of essential state;\n- missing and empty states are explicit;\n- focus and keyboard behavior match pointer behavior;\n- updates preserve keys, selection, and viewport state;\n- the chart remains readable at its smallest supported container;\n- a table or textual summary is available when exact values are essential.\n\nIf the first attempt fails one of these checks, change the representation\nbefore adding decorative complexity.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Choosing a Chart\ndescription: Choose a visual encoding from the question, data shape, and comparison task before composing TanStack Charts marks.\n---\n\nStart with the comparison a reader must make. A chart type is the result of\nthat decision, not the starting point.\n\nTanStack Charts does not inspect a dataset and choose a chart automatically.\nData exploration, cleaning, and recommendation belong in application tooling\nor an authoring skill. The runtime receives data that is ready to encode.\n\n## Task-first choices\n\n| Reader task                     | First choice                           | Common alternatives                                                     |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Follow change over ordered time | Line                                   | Area for accumulated magnitude; bars for discrete periods               |\n| Compare named categories        | Horizontal bars                        | Dots or lollipops when a zero baseline is not the message               |\n| Compare two values per category | Dumbbell or slopegraph                 | Grouped bars when absolute magnitude matters                            |\n| Inspect a relationship          | Scatterplot                            | Bubble scatterplot for a third quantitative value                       |\n| Inspect one distribution        | Histogram                              | ECDF when cumulative probability matters                                |\n| Compare distributions           | Boxplot or violin                      | Faceted histograms when shape details matter                            |\n| Show an interval                | Range area, interval bar, or error bar | Candlestick for financial open-high-low-close data                      |\n| Show composition                | Stacked bars or areas                  | Normalized stack for proportions; mosaic for two categorical dimensions |\n| Show a matrix                   | Heatmap                                | Labeled cells for small matrices                                        |\n| Repeat the same view by group   | Facets                                 | Linked views when each panel needs a different role                     |\n| Show topology                   | Node-link diagram                      | Matrix encoding when the network is dense                               |\n| Edit or navigate a time range   | Chart plus semantic controls           | Controlled brush or zoom behavior; application-owned scrubber or editor |\n\nThe [chart examples](../examples/index.md) show these choices as executable\ncompositions.\n\n## Check the data shape\n\nBefore choosing marks, identify:\n\n- the observation represented by one row;\n- quantitative, temporal, ordinal, and nominal fields;\n- whether missing values mean unknown, zero, or a deliberate gap;\n- whether order is semantic or merely presentational;\n- which rows share a series or group;\n- whether values are points or intervals;\n- whether aggregation would answer the question more clearly than raw marks.\n\nKeep those rows in their natural application shape. Use\n[channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md) to map fields or accessors into\nvisual properties. Do not convert data into a library-owned series structure.\n\n## Prefer the smallest complete composition\n\nUse this escalation order:\n\n1. One built-in mark or first-party composite mark.\n2. Several built-in marks sharing scales.\n3. Facets or explicitly linked views.\n4. D3-prepared rows passed to built-in marks.\n5. `compositeMark` for a reusable unit made only from ordinary marks.\n6. A custom mark that emits scene nodes.\n7. An application-owned overlay or gesture controller.\n\nPrefer a first-party mark when it owns inseparable semantics. `boxX` and `boxY`\naccept raw observations and keep quartiles, fences, whiskers, outliers, and\nlineage aligned. A candlestick remains a useful link-plus-ranged-rectangle\ncomposition. A focus-and-context view is two charts sharing semantic state.\n\nSee [Marks and Layering](../concepts/marks-and-layering.md),\n[Faceting and Composition](./faceting-and-composition.md), and\n[Custom Marks and Renderers](./custom-marks-and-renderers.md) for the\ncorresponding boundaries.\n\n## Avoid misleading defaults\n\n- Bar charts should normally include zero on the quantitative axis.\n- Line charts imply an ordered continuum. Do not connect unordered categories.\n- Area encodings compare distance from a baseline. Use explicit interval\n  endpoints when the baseline is not zero.\n- Stacks make totals easy to compare but interior layers harder to compare.\n- Bubble radius must use an area-preserving radial scale.\n- Dual quantitative axes often make unrelated movement look related. Prefer\n  aligned small multiples.\n- Three-dimensional effects distort position, length, and area. Keep analytical\n  marks planar.\n- More raw marks are not automatically more honest. For dense data, choose a\n  bounded representation that preserves the question.\n\nThe [Large Data](./large-data.md) guide covers the raw-versus-encoded decision.\n\n## Verify the result\n\nA chart is ready when:\n\n- its title or surrounding text states the question;\n- the axes and legend identify units;\n- ordering and aggregation are intentional;\n- color is not the only carrier of essential state;\n- missing and empty states are explicit;\n- focus and keyboard behavior match pointer behavior;\n- updates preserve keys, selection, and viewport state;\n- the chart remains readable at its smallest supported container;\n- a table or textual summary is available when exact values are essential.\n\nIf the first attempt fails one of these checks, change the representation\nbefore adding decorative complexity.\n"}
